@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Rollback Strategies in Azure DevOps";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="lead text-center">Learn how to implement rollback strategies to ensure stability and reliability in your deployments.</p>
    <hr />

    <!-- Introduction -->
    <div class="mt-4">
        <h2 class="text-success">What is a Rollback Strategy?</h2>
        <p>
            A rollback strategy is a predefined approach to reverting a deployment when an issue is detected in a production environment. 
            It helps maintain application stability, ensuring minimal downtime and mitigating risks associated with new releases.
        </p>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="https://example.com/rollback-strategy.jpg" alt="Rollback Strategies Overview" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg w-100">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Rollback strategies help maintain application availability and reliability.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Common Rollback Strategies -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-warning">Common Rollback Strategies</h2>
        <p>There are several rollback strategies to handle deployment failures:</p>
        <ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Revert to Previous Deployment:</strong> Restore the last stable version of the application.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Blue-Green Deployment:</strong> Maintain two environments and switch traffic to the stable one in case of failure.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Canary Rollback:</strong> Gradually roll back an update if issues arise in early-stage deployments.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Feature Flags:</strong> Disable problematic features dynamically without redeploying.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Database Versioning:</strong> Ensure database changes can be rolled back without data loss.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="https://example.com/rollback-methods.jpg" alt="Rollback Methods" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg w-100">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Different rollback strategies can be applied based on deployment needs.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step-by-Step Guide to Implementing Rollbacks in Azure DevOps -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-danger">Implementing Rollback Strategies in Azure DevOps</h2>
        <ol class="list-group list-group-numbered">
            <li class="list-group-item">Ensure all releases are version-controlled and tagged.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Use deployment slots in Azure App Services for quick rollback.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Create rollback tasks in Azure Pipelines for automated reversion.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Maintain backups of database schema and configurations.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Test rollback procedures regularly to ensure they function correctly.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="https://example.com/rollback-steps.jpg" alt="Rollback Steps" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg w-100">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Implementing rollback strategies ensures smooth recovery from failures.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Best Practices -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-info">Best Practices for Rollback Management</h2>
        <ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Automate Rollbacks:</strong> Use scripts and Azure DevOps pipelines to automate rollback processes.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Monitor Deployments:</strong> Use Application Insights to detect and alert on failures.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Maintain Backups:</strong> Regularly back up databases and configurations.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Test Rollbacks:</strong> Simulate rollback scenarios in staging before production use.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="https://example.com/rollback-best-practices.jpg" alt="Rollback Best Practices" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg w-100">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Following best practices ensures a reliable rollback process.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Conclusion -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-secondary">Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            Having a robust rollback strategy is essential for maintaining the stability of applications in production. 
            By implementing automated rollbacks, monitoring deployments, and using Azure DevOps tools, teams can minimize downtime and quickly recover from failures.
        </p>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="https://example.com/conclusion.jpg" alt="Rollback Strategies Summary" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg w-100">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Rollback strategies help mitigate risks and improve deployment reliability.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
